Preamble

  The licenses for most software are designed to take away your freedom to
share and change it.  By contrast, the GNU General Public License is intended
to guarantee your freedom to share and change free software--to make sure the
software is free for all its users.  This GNU General Public License refers
to the Free Software Foundation's licenses, and their spirit.

The purpose of this License is to make a software program free software, so
that everybody the way recipients can use it, modify it, know it was free
software, know their rights, and spread the software (and their knowledge
about it) to other people.  Furthermore, each author's reputation will not
be affected by subsequent modifications, or the misrepresentation of versions
of the author's work.  In order to achieve these objectives, we need to
prevent others from denying users these rights.  Furthermore, we insist that
modified versions be marked as changed, so that their problems will not be
attributed to authors of previous versions.  Some software instruments are
designed to deny users the ability to share and change it.  We guarantee
your right to share and change such software--to make sure it remains free
for all its users.  We believe that the way to carry out these objectives is
to have the license not be a property of any individual or corporation, but
be distributed under the terms of the GNU General Public License.

We permit redistribution and use of the source and compiled forms of the
code, with or without modification, under the terms of the GNU General
Public License.  We require that modified files carry prominent notices
that you changed the files.

